blocks.body:
Agriculture has been practised in India for thousands of years, and sustained use of land without matching technological and institutional changes hindered its development. Despite irrigation, most farmers still depend on the **monsoon** and natural fertility.

**Institutional reforms after Independence:** **collectivisation, consolidation of holdings, co-operation and abolition of zamindari** were given priority in the **First Five Year Plan**. Land reform was the main focus.

**Technological reforms:** the **Green Revolution**, based on the use of package technology (HYV seeds, fertilisers, irrigation), and the **White Revolution (Operation Flood)** for milk, were introduced in the 1960s and 1970s. But this led to the concentration of development in a few selected areas.

**Comprehensive land development programme** in the 1980s and 1990s included both institutional and technical reforms:
- provision of **crop insurance** against drought, flood, cyclone, fire and disease;
- establishment of **Grameen banks, co-operative societies and banks** for loans at lower rates of interest;
- **Kisan Credit Card (KCC)** and **Personal Accident Insurance Scheme (PAIS)**;
- special **weather bulletins and agricultural programmes** for farmers on radio and television;
- the government announces **minimum support price (MSP)**, remunerative and procurement prices for important crops to check exploitation of farmers by speculators and middlemen.

**Bhoodan-Gramdan:** **Vinoba Bhave**, a disciple of Gandhiji, walked across India. At Pochampalli (Andhra Pradesh, now Telangana), landless villagers asked for land, and a landowner offered 80 acres. This began the **Bhoodan** (gift of land) movement; some zamindars offered whole villages, called **Gramdan**. It is also known as the **'blood-less revolution'**.

